  "samplingHelpIntro": "Temperature and Top-P combine to set the amount of variety in Maia’s play.\n\nWe recommend Temperature 1.0, Top-P 0.95, and Max variety in opening enabled. These are the default settings. Based on our research, they offer a good balance of human-like play, opening variety and fewer unusual blunders.\n\nRead on to learn how these settings work.",
  "@samplingHelpIntro": {
    "description": "Engine help: Introduction and recommended defaults. Legacy message ID retained; English revised 2026-10-25. All supported languages translate this approved English explanation. Maia-specific, not a Lichess equivalent."
  },
  "samplingHelpVariety": "Temperature\n\nAt Temperature 0, Maia always plays the move it predicts a player at the selected Elo is most likely to play. This is technically called argmax.\n\nAt Temperature 1, Maia considers the full range of moves it predicts a player at the selected Elo might play, choosing among them according to their probabilities.\n\nAt a Temperature between 0 and 1, Maia puts more weight on the moves it considers most likely. Lower Temperature values make Maia's choices more predictable.\n\nFor example, in our tests with the 79M model at rating inputs of 1600 for both players, Maia’s opening probabilities as White were approximately:\n\n• 1. e4: 64%\n• 1. d4: 25%\n• 1. c4: 3%\n• All other moves combined: 8%\n\nAt Temperature 0, Maia always played 1. e4\n\nAt Temperature 0.5, it played 1. e4 about 86% of the time and 1. d4 about 13% of the time. Other moves remained possible, but together accounted for less than 1%.\n\nAt Temperature 1, Maia followed the original probabilities listed above.",
  "@samplingHelpVariety": {
    "description": "Engine help: Temperature section and opening probability examples. Legacy message ID retained; English revised 2026-10-25. All supported languages translate this approved English explanation. Maia-specific, not a Lichess equivalent."
  },
  "samplingHelpDefaults": "Top-P\n\nTop-P filters the available moves after Temperature adjusts their probabilities.\n\nAt Top-P 1, no filtering is applied. All available moves can be played according to their temperature-adjusted probabilities.\n\nAt Top-P 0.95, Maia keeps the most likely moves whose combined probability reaches at least 95%, then chooses among them. The excluded moves account for at most 5% of the probability.\n\nAt Top-P 0, Maia always chooses the most likely move. This has the same effect as Temperature 0: either setting is sufficient to produce argmax.\n\nIn our opening example, Temperature 1 and Top-P 0.60 would make Maia always play 1. e4. That move alone accounts for about 64% of the probability, so no other moves are needed to reach the 60% threshold.",
  "@samplingHelpDefaults": {
    "description": "Engine help: Top-P section and filtering example. Legacy message ID retained; English revised 2026-10-25. All supported languages translate this approved English explanation. Maia-specific, not a Lichess equivalent."
  },
  "samplingHelpStrength": "How do these settings affect Maia’s play?\n\nYou can think of Top-P as an angel on Maia’s shoulder. At 0, the angel tells Maia to ignore lower-probability moves, which can include blunders or unsound moves. At 1.0, the angel has gone, and Maia could select even the craziest, most improbable move.\n\nIn our direct-match tests at the 1600 setting, argmax outperformed Temperature 1.0 / Top-P 1.0 by the equivalent of about 300 Elo points. However, argmax also limited opening choices to a narrow range of mainly main lines.",
  "@samplingHelpStrength": {
    "description": "Engine help: Effect on play, analogy and direct-match research. Legacy message ID retained; English revised 2026-10-25. All supported languages translate this approved English explanation. Maia-specific, not a Lichess equivalent."
  },
  "samplingHelpArgmax": "So, we introduced Max variety in opening: a toggle which sets Maia to play with Temperature 1.0 and Top-P 1.0 for the first five moves by each side. You might see some crazy things in the opening phase! From move six, Maia switches to your configured settings.",
  "@samplingHelpArgmax": {
    "description": "Engine help: Opening variety override. Legacy message ID retained; English revised 2026-10-25. All supported languages translate this approved English explanation. Maia-specific, not a Lichess equivalent."
  },
  "samplingHelpTopP": "We recommend keeping max opening variety on and Temperature set to 1.0 and Top-P set to 0.95. This is like having a creative player with an angel on its shoulder that at least talks it out of playing the totally far-out moves—the least likely moves whose combined probability is up to 5%. These might be brilliant, true, but they can also be silly blunders. So this should make for a good balance of variety in the opening, creativity in the middle game, and stronger play through the exclusion of some very low-probability blunders.\n\nFor those feeling brave, Temperature 0 produced the strongest play in our tests. Good luck!",
  "@samplingHelpTopP": {
    "description": "Engine help: Recommended play and strongest tested setting. Legacy message ID retained; English revised 2026-10-25. All supported languages translate this approved English explanation. Maia-specific, not a Lichess equivalent."
  },
  "samplingHelpResearchIntro": "See the visual guide for the research behind these choices, with links to the full results.",
  "@samplingHelpResearchIntro": {
    "description": "Maia-specific sampling guidance for the 2.5 default proposal. Preserve probability mass versus individual move probability, the five-full-move boundary and conditional direct-match strength claims. Not a Lichess equivalent. Explanation selector changes prose only."
  },
  "samplingFullVarietyNotice": "Maia may play more unusual blunders with Temperature and Top-P at 1.0 / 1.0.",
  "@samplingFullVarietyNotice": {
    "description": "Maia-specific sampling guidance for the 2.5 default proposal. Preserve probability mass versus individual move probability, the five-full-move boundary and conditional direct-match strength claims. Not a Lichess equivalent. Explanation selector changes prose only."
